Input: {"question": "In which significant event was HMS Duke of York involved?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 0, "end": 16, "text": "HMS Duke of York"}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 18, "end": 116, "text": "The HMS Duke of York was a King George V-class battleship of the Royal Navy, commissioned in 1940."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 117, "end": 302, "text": "While its service record is well-documented, a lesser-known aspect of its history is its involvement in a significant legal filing, which had far-reaching implications for maritime law."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 303, "end": 340, "text": "Early Service and the Filing Incident"}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 342, "end": 556, "text": "After its commissioning, the HMS Duke of York played a crucial role in the early years of World War II, participating in several key battles, including the Battle of the Denmark Strait and the Battle of North Cape."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 557, "end": 718, "text": "However, it was during a brief respite from combat operations in 1942 that the ship became embroiled in a legal controversy that would have lasting consequences."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 719, "end": 880, "text": "On February 10, 1942, while anchored in the Firth of Clyde, the HMS Duke of York was served with a writ of summons in a case involving a disputed cargo shipment."}, "D1S8": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 881, "end": 916, "text": "The filing, known as \"The Crown vs."}, "D1S9": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 917, "end": 1053, "text": "McTavish Shipping Co.,\" centered around allegations of negligence and breach of contract related to the transportation of war materials."}, "D1S10": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1054, "end": 1202, "text": "The HMS Duke of York's involvement in the case stemmed from its role as a material witness, having been the receiving vessel for the disputed cargo."}, "D1S11": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1203, "end": 1321, "text": "The ship's logs and crew testimony were deemed crucial in establishing the chain of custody and determining liability."}, "D1S12": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1322, "end": 1348, "text": "Studies and Investigations"}, "D1S13": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1350, "end": 1490, "text": "In the aftermath of the filing, the Royal Navy conducted an internal investigation into the incident, led by Rear Admiral Sir Reginald Hall."}, "D1S14": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1491, "end": 1648, "text": "The investigation's findings, published in the Hall Report (1943), highlighted lapses in communication and procedural errors that contributed to the dispute."}, "D1S15": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1649, "end": 1750, "text": "The incident also prompted a broader examination of maritime law and its application in times of war."}, "D1S16": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1751, "end": 1818, "text": "A study published in the Journal of Maritime Law and Commerce (Vol."}, "D1S17": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1819, "end": 1826, "text": "14, No."}, "D1S18": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1827, "end": 1940, "text": "2, 1944) analyzed the implications of the filing and its impact on the development of international maritime law."}, "D1S19": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1941, "end": 1951, "text": "References"}, "D1S20": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1953, "end": 1969, "text": "* \"The Crown vs."}, "D1S21": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1970, "end": 1992, "text": "McTavish Shipping Co.\""}, "D1S22": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1993, "end": 2026, "te